    "notes": "Live hazard events from the keyless GDACS event API, one row per event. Included: DR (Drought), FL (Flood), TC (Tropical Cyclone) — the hazard classes that move food security directly, through lost production (drought), drowned crops and cut feeder roads (flood), and flattened pre-harvest crops and closed ports (tropical cyclone). Omitted: EQ (Earthquake), WF (Wildfire), VO (Volcano) — earthquake is available behind FOODSHIELD_GDACS_TYPES but is off by default because its food impact runs through logistics already covered by LPI and the trade layers, and it is frequent enough to swamp the map; wildfire measures burned forest hectares, a poor cropland proxy, and remains a genuine coverage gap left by the dead NASA FIRMS feed. Alert levels Orange and Red only — GDACS Green means no significant impact expected and outnumbers the rest ~15:1. Window: events whose end date falls within the last 90 days, or which have not ended. is_current is derived here from the event dates (end date not more than 7 days past, or still in the future), NOT from GDACS's own iscurrent flag, which means 'in the live homepage feed' and reads false for droughts that are running today; their flag is preserved as gdacs_iscurrent. severity_score (0-100) is a rendering weight derived from the GDACS alert band, the only cross-hazard-comparable figure in the feed; the raw severity_value carries incompatible units per type (km2 for drought, magnitude for quake, km/h for cyclone, hectares for fire, and nothing at all for flood) and must never be compared across types. population_affected is null on every row: GDACS publishes no consistent affected-population figure in this API — the Sendai records mix 'out of contact' and 'rescued' counts into the 'affected' tag and the RSS population field mixes deaths, displaced and shaking-intensity exposure — so no number is published rather than an invented one. Counts: DR=4, FL=6, TC=7. 7 current.",
    "version": "v23",
    "status": "ok"
  },
